
1. Temporal Dead Zone (TDZ)
The Temporal Dead Zone (TDZ) is the time between when a variable is declared and when it is initialized.

let and const variables cannot be used before they are declared.

console.log(name); // Error

let name = "Sejal";
Why?

Because let and const stay in the Temporal Dead Zone until the declaration line is reached.

2. Hoisting
Hoisting means JavaScript moves declarations to the top of their scope before execution.

var
console.log(a);

var a = 10;
Output

undefined
Because JavaScript treats it like:

var a;

console.log(a);

a = 10;
let
console.log(a);

let a = 10;
Output

ReferenceError
Because of the Temporal Dead Zone.

3. A Variable Cannot Be Declared Twice
Using let
let age = 20;
let age = 25;
Output

SyntaxError
Using const
const PI = 3.14;
const PI = 3.1415;
Also gives an error.

Using var
var age = 20;
var age = 25;

console.log(age);
Output

25
var allows redeclaration.

