14. this Keyword
this refers to the object that is currently calling the function (the current object context).

Example

const person = {
    name: "Sejal",

    greet() {
        console.log(this.name);
    }
};

person.greet();
Output

Sejal
Here, this.name means person.name.

15. Why Use Objects?
Objects group related data together.

Without an object

let name = "Sejal";
let age = 20;
let city = "Kathmandu";
These variables are separate.

With an object

const student = {
    name: "Sejal",
    age: 20,
    city: "Kathmandu"
};
Everything related to the student is stored together.

Access values

console.log(student.name);
console.log(student.age);
16. Destructuring
Meaning in English
Destructuring means taking values out of an object or an array and storing them in separate variables easily.

Instead of writing:

const student = {
    name: "Sejal",
    age: 20,
    city: "Kathmandu"
};

const name = student.name;
const age = student.age;

console.log(name);
console.log(age);
You can use destructuring:

const student = {
    name: "Sejal",
    age: 20,
    city: "Kathmandu"
};

const { name, age } = student;

console.log(name);
console.log(age);
Output

Sejal
20
Renaming Variables
const student = {
    name: "Sejal",
    age: 20
};

const { name: studentName, age: studentAge } = student;

console.log(studentName);
console.log(studentAge);
Output

Sejal
20
Array Destructuring
const colors = ["Red", "Green", "Blue"];

const [first, second] = colors;

console.log(first);
console.log(second);
Output

Red
Green
Simple Definition for Exams
Destructuring: A JavaScript feature that extracts values from objects or arrays and assigns them to variables in a shorter and cleaner way.


closure means

A closure is a JavaScript feature where an inner function remembers and can access the variables of its outer function even after the outer function has finished executing.

Simple Definition (Exam)
Closure: A closure is created when a function remembers and can use variables from its outer (lexical) scope, even after the outer function has returned.

Example 1
function outer() {
    let count = 0;

    function inner() {
        count++;
        console.log(count);
    }

    return inner;
}

const counter = outer();

counter(); // 1
counter(); // 2
counter(); // 3
How it works
outer() is called.

count is created with value 0.

outer() returns the inner function.

Normally, count would disappear after outer() finishes.

But because inner still uses count, JavaScript keeps it alive.

Every time counter() is called, it remembers the same count variable.

Example 2
function greet(name) {

    function sayHello() {
        console.log("Hello " + name);
    }

    return sayHello;
}

const helloSejal = greet("Sejal");

helloSejal();
Output

Hello Sejal
Even though greet() has finished executing, sayHello() still remembers the value of name.

Why are Closures Useful?
Closures are commonly used for:

Data privacy (private variables)

Counters

Callbacks

Event handlers

Function factories

Memoization

Real-world Counter Example
function createCounter() {
    let count = 0;

    return function () {
        count++;
        return count;
    };
}

const counter = createCounter();

console.log(counter()); // 1
console.log(counter()); // 2
console.log(counter()); // 3
Here, count is private. It cannot be accessed directly:

console.log(count); // Error
Only the returned function can access and modify it.

One-Line Memory Trick
Closure = Function + Remembered Outer Variables

A closure allows a function to remember the variables from its outer scope, even after the outer function has finished executing.


